V3DRender 0.12: kit 14, memory above 1 GB first
================================================

RISC3D now loads V3DRender at start-up, so it is always there when
Reel plays a video with ReelHWAccel. The VideoCore (which decodes and
encodes H.264 for ReelHWAccel, through VCHIQ) can only reach the first
1 GB of RAM, and needs contiguous memory there. The V3D can reach all
of it, so V3DRender 0.12 takes its memory from above 1 GB where it can.

What's new to look at:
  - *V3DRender now shows "above 1 GB" and "below 1 GB" under buffer
    memory, and where the GPU's page table is. On a Pi with more than
    1 GB, "below 1 GB" should stay 0 and the page table should say
    "(above 1 GB)".
  - Check's new test 12 makes an 8 MB buffer and reads where every
    page is: all of them should be above 1 GB.

Please send Results (from Check), and *V3DRender's output. If you can,
play an H.264 video in Reel with hardware decoding while V3DRender is
loaded, then run *V3DRender again.

Everything from kit 13 (test 11's read-back figures) is unchanged.

Before you start
- A Pi 4, Pi 400 or CM4 with RISC OS 5.30 or later. V3DDriver not
  loaded. Copy this folder to a hard disc.

The test
1. Double-click Replay. It loads V3DRender 0.12 from this folder, in
   place of an older one. No program should be using the GPU at the same time:
   Replay needs the buffer addresses Mesa's job had.
2. Optional: double-click Check too (kits 1-5's tests, all passed in
   kit 5).
3. Send Results back.
